[Music]  one of the most common themes  in my work as an executive coach is  what i call essentially people problems  you know almost all my clients  are our ceos um the vast majority of  them are  are leaders of some group of people and  the dilemma that they face that they  want to work with me on is is you know  the difficulties that they're  experiencing  you know in some cases with the people  they lead and manage in other cases with  their peers  in in other cases with uh investors or  other  uh stakeholders or authority figures and  and in that work i'm reminded  of a great line from the eminent french  film director jean renoir  and he says the the hell of life is that  everyone has their reasons  and and that's really important to  remind ourselves of when we're when we  are met with somebody whose behavior we  find you know frustrating or maddening  or confusing uh you know we we often  leap to some assumptions  about the fact that they're um uh  you know we we assume that that they're  that they lack understanding we assume  that they're you know that they're  acting out of you know spite or  capriciousness  you know we make a whole host of  assumptions which often uh you know  justify our own perspective  and are typically very unempathetic and  what we  fail to do habitually is stop and and  ask ourselves  what is that person's reason you know i  find their behavior  maddening or frustrating they're getting  in the way it somehow  and what we need to do is slow ourselves  down and remind ourselves  everyone has their reasons very rarely  in life  do we actually do we encounter an actual  social you know very rarely do we  encounter somebody who's deliberately  going out of their way to screw things  up and  and make things difficult for us it's  much more often the case  almost universally the case that when we  find ourselves in conflict  um it's about misunderstandings and and  and missed expectations  and incorrect assumptions and so so much  of my work  entails encouraging people to slow down  examine the assumptions that they're  making about the other person  develop a theory of mind what is what is  governing this other person what are  their beliefs and assumptions what are  their goals  and can i allow that information to  inform how i approach them  so rather than assuming you know they're  uh you know they're they're coming here  to  you know they're they're taking these  steps and actions to kind of mess me up  and get in my way  um can i take a broader perspective on  what might be  driving them what what impulses or  reasoning  might be informing their behavior it's  also important to bear in mind that in  some sense  all behavior is adaptive and so when we  run into  behavior that we find frustrating or  confusing it's important to ask  ourselves  well what are they adapting to and for a  leader it's  especially important to ask you know are  they adapting to me  am i in some sense creating a condition  that they're  finding the need to adapt to that that  you know i might find frustrating so is  there something i  can do to change how i'm showing up and  influence  the environment in which this other  person is operating  you

